    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Vitamin D deficiency is usually silent, and when it does cause symptoms they are diffuse bone pain in the lower back, hips and thighs, proximal muscle weakness such as difficulty rising from a chair, and fatigue.</strong> Only a 25(OH)D blood test confirms it: across 2.2 million Indian test results, 46.5% were deficient.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The classic signs:</strong> bone pain and bone tenderness in the lower spine, pelvis, hips and ribs, with weakness in the thigh and shoulder muscles. In one series of 17 biopsy-proven osteomalacia cases, bone pain with muscle weakness was present in 94% and tenderness in 88%.</li>
        <li><strong>The thresholds most Indian labs use:</strong> below 20 ng/mL is deficient, 21 to 29 ng/mL is insufficient, and below 10 to 12 ng/mL is severe.</li>
        <li><strong>Correction is a prescription, not a multivitamin:</strong> the usual Indian schedule is 60,000 IU of D3 once weekly for six to eight weeks, then maintenance. Skelecoll's 600 IU is the ICMR-NIN maintenance allowance, not a correction dose.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limitation:</strong> these symptoms are non-specific, and supplements are not a painkiller. In the VITAL trial, 2,000 IU daily for a median 5.3 years did not reduce fractures, with a hazard ratio of 0.98, even in people who started below 20 ng/mL.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

