      <h2 id="symptoms">What are the symptoms of vitamin D deficiency?</h2>

      <p>
        In most people, none. That is the first thing worth saying, because it is the one sentence
        the symptom lists leave out. A review arguing against over-testing and over-treatment puts
        it plainly: in most cases vitamin D deficiency is almost asymptomatic. Plenty of people
        sitting at 15 ng/mL feel completely well.
      </p>

      <p>
        When deficiency does declare itself, it does so through bone and muscle, and the pattern is
        recognisable. Adults develop osteomalacia, which is softening of bone from poor
        mineralisation. The clinical features reported across reviews and case series are:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Diffuse bone pain, not joint pain.</strong> It sits in the lower spine, pelvis, hips, ribs, thighs and feet, and it is usually described as a deep ache rather than a sharp pain.</li>
        <li><strong>Bone tenderness.</strong> Pressing on the shin, ribs or pelvis hurts. This is a useful discriminator, because mechanical knee or back pain does not do that.</li>
        <li><strong>Proximal muscle weakness.</strong> Weakness in the muscles closest to the trunk. The practical version is difficulty rising from a chair or a bed without using the arms, and difficulty climbing stairs. In severe cases the gait changes to a waddle.</li>
        <li><strong>Fatigue and a general sense of being unwell,</strong> which is real but far too non-specific to diagnose on.</li>
        <li><strong>Fractures from minor trauma,</strong> in the more advanced cases. In the 17-case biopsy-proven series, 76% had already fractured by the time the diagnosis was made.</li>
        <li><strong>Rickets in children,</strong> with bowed legs, delayed walking and a widening of the wrists. This is a different and more urgent presentation, and it belongs to a paediatrician.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        The diagnostic trap is that all of this is vague early on. Reviews of osteomalacia note it
        escapes recognition precisely because the early symptoms are indefinite, and that
        polymyalgia rheumatica, myositis and fibromyalgia sit in the same differential. In my own
        practice the more common error runs the other way: a patient with ten years of
        <a href="/pages/supplements-for-knee-pain">mechanical knee pain</a> gets a vitamin D result
        of 18 ng/mL, corrects it, and is disappointed when the knee still hurts. Both things were
        true. Only one of them was causing the pain.
      </p>

      <h2 id="levels">What vitamin D level counts as a deficiency?</h2>

      <p>
        Most Indian labs and consensus statements use below 20 ng/mL for deficiency and 21 to 29
        ng/mL for insufficiency, which come from the Endocrine Society's 2011 guideline. Those
        numbers are convention rather than settled science, and in 2024 the Endocrine Society
        itself stopped endorsing them.
      </p>

      <div class="jr-table-scroll">
        <table class="jr-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">25(OH)D result</th><th scope="col">Label on most Indian reports</th><th scope="col">What it usually means</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Below 10 to 12 ng/mL</th><td>Severe deficiency</td><td>The band reviews associate with rickets, osteomalacia and diffuse bone pain. Needs a doctor now.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Below 20 ng/mL (50 nmol/L)</th><td>Deficient</td><td>The 2011 Endocrine Society cut-off, still used across Indian practice. Often symptomless.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">21 to 29 ng/mL</th><td>Insufficient</td><td>Very common. This is the band where the guidelines disagree with each other most.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">30 ng/mL and above</th><td>Sufficient</td><td>No trial evidence that pushing the number higher than this buys anything extra.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The 2024 Endocrine Society guideline on vitamin D for disease prevention explicitly no
        longer endorses specific 25(OH)D values to define sufficiency, insufficiency and deficiency.
        Its stated reason is that the trial evidence does not let anyone set a threshold that
        predicts who will actually benefit from supplementation. It is an uncomfortable position for
        an industry built on a number, and it is the most honest reading of the data available.
      </p>

      <h2 id="india">Why is vitamin D deficiency so common in India despite the sunshine?</h2>

      <p>
        Because sunlight only makes vitamin D under conditions that most urban Indian life does not
        meet. Melanin-rich skin absorbs UVB, which is the wavelength the skin needs, so the same
        exposure produces less vitamin D than it would in a paler-skinned population. The hours when
        UVB is strong enough to matter, roughly 10am to 3pm, are also the hours almost everyone
        spends indoors. Clothing that covers most of the skin and routine sunscreen use both cut it
        further. In Delhi and Mumbai in winter, smog reduces the UVB that reaches street level.
      </p>

      <p>
        Diet does not make up the gap. Vitamin D occurs in very few foods, India eats little oily
        fish, and fortified products are still uncommon on most shelves. A review in the
        <em>Journal of Family Medicine and Primary Care</em> lists exactly this combination:
        pigmentation, sunscreen, pollution and changing food habits producing low calcium and
        vitamin D intake together.
      </p>

