      <p>
        Be careful with the headline numbers. The 46.5% figure above comes from people who had a
        reason to be tested. Community estimates run far lower, with one 2025 analysis reporting
        34.3% in urban areas and 13% in rural areas, while a 2025 paper in the <em>Indian Journal of
        Public Health</em> cites an estimated prevalence of 80% to 90%. That spread is not a mystery:
        it is what happens when different studies use different cut-offs and different populations.
        The only number that applies to you is your own.
      </p>

      <h2 id="test">Should you get a vitamin D test?</h2>

      <p>
        If you have the bone and muscle symptoms above, yes, and a 25(OH)D test is the only way to
        confirm it. If you feel well and are simply curious, the guidelines have turned against it.
        The 2024 Endocrine Society guideline suggests against routine 25(OH)D screening in healthy
        people, including in people with obesity and in people with darker skin, and also against
        routine repeat testing to guide dosing.
      </p>

      <p>
        That recommendation is for healthy adults and it is not a reason to ignore a symptom. It
        exists because screening a well population generates a lot of borderline results, a lot of
        unnecessary prescriptions and occasionally real harm. Your doctor will usually test when
        there is a reason: unexplained bone pain, proximal weakness, a fragility fracture, a
        <a href="/pages/how-to-increase-bone-density">low DEXA result</a>, malabsorption, long-term
        steroid use, bariatric surgery, chronic kidney or liver disease, or pregnancy.
      </p>

      <p>
        The same guideline does recommend higher-than-RDA vitamin D for four groups without testing
        them first: children and adolescents aged 1 to 18, adults aged 75 and over, people who are
        pregnant, and adults with prediabetes. For healthy adults under 75 it advises against going
        beyond the recommended daily allowance unless intake or sun exposure is genuinely
        inadequate.
      </p>

      <h2 id="fix">How much vitamin D does it take to correct a deficiency?</h2>

      <p>
        Far more than any food supplement contains, and for a defined period. The distinction
        between a correction dose and a maintenance dose is the single most useful thing on this
        page, and it is the one almost no product label explains.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Correction, if a test confirms deficiency:</strong> the standard Indian schedule is 60,000 IU of cholecalciferol once a week. The 2025 Indian expert consensus in the <em>Indian Journal of Endocrinology and Metabolism</em> recommends 60,000 IU weekly for six weeks in children aged three and over; for adults, sources describe six to eight weeks before moving to maintenance. Your doctor decides the length, and this is a prescription, not a shelf purchase.</li>
        <li><strong>Why the big dose:</strong> in a small comparative trial, 60,000 IU weekly raised mean serum 25(OH)D by 28.33 ng/mL over ten weeks, against 6.79 ng/mL on 1,000 IU daily. Small daily doses correct a true deficiency very slowly.</li>
        <li><strong>Maintenance, afterwards:</strong> the ICMR-NIN 2020 recommended daily allowance for Indian adults is 600 IU, with an estimated average requirement of 400 IU. This is the amount that keeps a corrected level steady.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not improvise the big dose.</strong> The commonly cited tolerable upper intake level is 4,000 IU a day for anyone over the age of one, though adult figures differ between papers. Weekly 60,000 IU sachets are safe for a short prescribed course and are not safe as a permanent habit.</li>
        <li><strong>Retest only if your doctor asks.</strong> The 2024 guideline advises against routine follow-up testing to titrate a dose.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Vitamin D is fat-soluble, so it absorbs better taken with a meal that contains some fat than
        on an empty stomach. Beyond that, consistency matters more than clock time, which is the
        same conclusion the trials on <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">collagen for
        joints</a> keep reaching.
      </p>

