      <h2 id="limits">What vitamin D will not do</h2>

      <p>
        Correcting a deficiency is worth doing. Taking vitamin D in the hope that it does something
        else is where the evidence stops cooperating, and three large results are worth knowing
        before you buy anything.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Fractures.</strong> The VITAL fracture analysis, published in the
        <em>New England Journal of Medicine</em> in 2022, gave 25,871 adults 2,000 IU of D3 daily or
        placebo for a median of 5.3 years. The hazard ratio for total fractures was 0.98 (95% CI
        0.89 to 1.08, P = 0.70), with null results for non-vertebral and hip fractures too, and no
        benefit even in participants who started below 20 ng/mL. The Australian D-Health trial, using
        60,000 IU monthly, found the same thing: no effect on fracture risk overall, hazard ratio
        0.94 (95% CI 0.84 to 1.06). Note that neither trial recruited people with established
        deficiency, so this is evidence against supplementing the already-replete, not evidence
        against treating a real deficiency.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Muscle strength.</strong> A 2018 meta-analysis of 12 randomised trials in
        postmenopausal women found no significant effect of vitamin D supplementation on hand grip
        strength, back strength or the Timed Up and Go test. A review of trials in athletes reached a
        similar verdict: serum levels rose, strength did not reliably follow. If you want
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-men">stronger tendons and better recovery</a>, the load you put
        through them is doing the work, not the capsule.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Falls.</strong> Here the picture is genuinely mixed. One meta-analysis of 32
        randomised trials reported a reduced risk of falls, with a pooled relative risk of 0.91
        (95% CI 0.85 to 0.98), while a more recent review in community-dwelling older adults found
        only a non-significant trend. The benefit, where it appears, clusters in people who started
        out deficient. That is a consistent theme across this whole literature: vitamin D helps
        people who are short of it, and does very little for people who are not.
      </p>

