      <h2 id="doctor">When to see a doctor</h2>

      <p>
        Several of the symptoms above overlap with conditions that a supplement cannot touch. Book an
        appointment rather than starting a vitamin, if you have:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>Bone pain with bone tenderness, particularly in the ribs, pelvis or shins.</li>
        <li>New difficulty standing up from a chair or climbing stairs, or a change in how you walk.</li>
        <li>A fracture from a minor fall or knock, which needs investigating in its own right.</li>
        <li>A child with bowed legs, delayed walking, or persistent leg pain. This is urgent and paediatric.</li>
        <li>Numbness, tingling or muscle cramps and spasms, which can point to low calcium alongside low vitamin D.</li>
        <li>Nausea, vomiting, excessive thirst or passing a lot of urine while taking vitamin D, which are the reported features of vitamin D toxicity and need a same-week review.</li>
        <li>Chronic kidney disease, liver disease, sarcoidosis, malabsorption, or long-term steroid use, all of which change how vitamin D should be dosed.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        That last caution is not theoretical. Indian case series describe real harm from unsupervised
        and over-enthusiastic dosing. A Delhi-NCR tertiary centre reported 16 patients whose vitamin
        D toxicity was caused by overdose in every single case, at a median cumulative dose of around
        3.6 million IU. A prospective series from Srinagar found 32 cases in which every patient had
        received multiple intramuscular vitamin D injections, and reported a median time to
        resolution of the resulting hypercalcaemia of seven months. The dose that corrects a
        deficiency and the dose that causes a problem are closer together than most people assume.
      </p>

