        speak to a qualified healthcare professional before starting supplementation.
      </p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>What are the first signs of vitamin D deficiency?</summary>
          <p>Usually there are none. Reviews describe vitamin D deficiency as almost asymptomatic in most cases. When early signs do appear they are a deep, diffuse ache in the lower back, hips and thighs, tenderness when the shin or ribs are pressed, tiredness, and difficulty rising from a chair without using the arms.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is a normal vitamin D level in India?</summary>
          <p>Most Indian labs report 30 ng/mL and above as sufficient, 21 to 29 ng/mL as insufficient, below 20 ng/mL as deficient and below 10 to 12 ng/mL as severe. Those cut-offs come from the Endocrine Society's 2011 guideline. The same society withdrew its endorsement of specific thresholds in 2024, so treat them as useful convention rather than settled fact.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long does it take to correct a vitamin D deficiency?</summary>
          <p>Six to eight weeks on a prescribed weekly dose is the usual Indian schedule. The 2025 Indian expert consensus recommends 60,000 IU once weekly for six weeks in children aged three and over, and adult courses are commonly described as six to eight weeks before switching to maintenance. In one small trial, 60,000 IU weekly raised serum levels by 28.33 ng/mL in ten weeks against 6.79 ng/mL on 1,000 IU daily.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can vitamin D deficiency cause joint pain and knee pain?</summary>
          <p>It causes bone pain rather than true joint pain, and the two are often confused. Severe deficiency is linked with osteomalacia, which produces diffuse aching in the spine, pelvis, hips and thighs along with bone tenderness. Mechanical knee pain and osteoarthritis have their own causes, and correcting a vitamin D level will not resolve them on its own.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is 600 IU of vitamin D a day enough?</summary>
          <p>It is enough to maintain a level that is already normal, and not enough to correct a confirmed deficiency. 600 IU is the ICMR-NIN 2020 recommended daily allowance for Indian adults, which is also what one 30ml Skelecoll shot supplies. If a blood test shows deficiency, that needs a doctor's prescribed course first, with 600 IU a day as the maintenance amount afterwards.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much sunlight do I need for vitamin D in India?</summary>
          <p>There is no reliable number, which is why the current guidelines no longer rely on sun estimates. The useful window is roughly 10am to 3pm when UVB is strong enough, but how much vitamin D your skin makes depends on skin tone, latitude, season, cloud cover, air pollution and how much skin is uncovered. Melanin-rich skin produces less from the same exposure.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can you take too much vitamin D?</summary>
          <p>Yes, and Indian hospitals see it. The commonly cited tolerable upper intake level is 4,000 IU a day for anyone over the age of one. One Delhi-NCR series reported 16 toxicity cases all caused by overdose, at a median cumulative dose of about 3.6 million IU, and a Srinagar series of 32 cases all involved repeated intramuscular injections. Symptoms include vomiting, excessive thirst and passing a lot of urine.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does vitamin D deficiency cause hair fall and fatigue?</summary>
          <p>Fatigue is a commonly reported symptom, but it is far too non-specific to diagnose on, and anaemia, thyroid disease and poor sleep are more likely explanations. Hair fall has much weaker evidence tying it to vitamin D specifically; iron and ferritin, thyroid function and protein intake matter more. A blood test, not a symptom checklist, is what settles it.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

